From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 22:18:23 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: chunked KDA prompt kernel, prompt grid and
 prompt replay, TF_GLM_KDA_CHUNKED (MiaAI-Lab recipe patches 0012, 0014, 0039,
 0008, 0042)

MiaAI-Lab's recipe (github.com/MiaAI-Lab/GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3-2x-DGX-Sparks-TensorFold @ 1576746, with KDA_CHUNKED=1
in its config) runs GLM-5.3-Flash's linear-attention (KDA) prompt chunks in chunked (WY) form: one CUDA kernel of
32-row sub-chunks at absolute positions, the 128 x 128 state carried in registers. Its arithmetic is close to the
serial kernel's, not the same bits ("50k prefill 29.3 -> 26.4 s"); a row's bits depend only on its sub-chunk and the
state entering it, so prompt chunks and kept prompt states sit on a 64-token grid, and an identical prompt replays
its kept end state. This ports the end state of recipe patches 0012-glm-kda-chunked, 0014-glm-kda-chunked-gb10 and
0039-glm-kda-chunked-kernel, with 0008-glm-prompt-grid and 0042-glm-prompt-replay (Apache License 2.0, Copyright 2026
MiaAI-Lab):

- kda_chunk.cu / kda_chunk.cpp (new): the recipe's kernel and binding, byte for byte (a two-line credit header added).
  Built as tensorfold_glm_kda_chunk with -O3 --fmad=false, like kda.cu.
- kda_chunked.py (new): the recipe's torch references (prep_reference, recurrent_reference, chunked_reference,
  gate_norm_reference, chain_reference) verbatim, and chain, which runs the kernel. The recipe file defines
  chain_reference twice; the first, shadowed one is dropped.
- kda.py: TF_GLM_KDA_CHUNKED; chain(..., pos=) sends a prompt chunk to the chunked kernel under the setting; decode
  windows keep the serial kernels. prompt_kw(pos) gives a prompt chunk's position only under the setting, so without
  it forward.py's calls (kda_rows, and this tree's kept-cut path inside a chunk) are the ones before.
- engine.py: kda_chunked_on, GRIDS, prompt_grid (TF_GLM_PROMPT_GRID; 64 under TF_GLM_KDA_CHUNKED=1, read before the
  AFD wire opens), grid_point, keep_at; both ranks must agree on the grid and the setting; startup lines. With a grid
  the single-stream engine keeps a drafted prompt's state at its grid point (prefilling to it as a prompt of that
  length, no cut inside a chunk), an on-grid prompt's end state keeps the head's logits row, and _resume takes an
  on-grid state or the whole prompt with its row (a replay). Shared-prefix points round down to the grid.
- decode.py: Snapshot.head, whole, prefill's replay (the state goes back, the first token is sampled from the kept
  row, nothing prefilled; an MTP stream's last hidden row waits for the first token) and its grid check.
- multi.py (PR #243's engine): the same with a grid: Lane.head, keep_at's points, _resume, _keep(head=), the replay
  FILL, fill_rows on the grid; multi-prompt chunks leave replays out.

Without a grid nothing changes: a drafted prompt keeps its state before its last token, and no kept state carries a
head row. TF_GLM_KDA_CHUNKED and TF_GLM_PROMPT_GRID are off unless set (the recipe's config turns KDA_CHUNKED on);
unset, the replies, cached counts, kept states and pool rows are the ones before, and the forward's kernel calls are
the same. On the attention/expert split this is attention-node only: the expert nodes and the wire are unchanged. The
new CUDA module needs an entry wherever the extensions are prebuilt.

Tests: tests/test_glm_kda_chunked.py (CPU: the references pinned to the recipe's source; chunked equals the delta
rule in float64; a cut on the 32-row grid keeps the bits, one elsewhere does not; kda.chain's and kda_chunked.chain's
dispatch; the forward's recorded calls with and without the setting; the settings). tests/test_glm_prompt_grid.py
(CPU, PR #243's fake model at a 64-token grid: grid points, resumes, replays prefilling nothing, next turns, at two
ranks with DFlash2 and MTP drafts, at world 1 over the fake AFD wire, with TF_GLM_KV=fp8's rings, shared-prefix
points, multi-prompt chunks, and the single-stream engine; replies equal their requests served alone).
tests/cuda/test_glm_kda_chunked.py (GPU: the kernel against the references, determinism, the grid cut's bits).

Credit: THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md ("GLM chunked KDA prompt kernel, prompt grid and prompt replay") and the ported
functions' comments name MiaAI-Lab and the patches.

Picked onto the joined head with prompt chunk pairs (sliced fills 0062, decode kernels 0016, draft policies
0021, tool calls, prompt chunk pairs): the conflicts keep both sides, and a replay never starts a sliced fill
(`lane.head is None`: a replay has no prompt rows), as in MiaAI-Lab's 0062 end state. A pair's second chunk
keeps a prompt's end state with its head row on the grid, as `_fill` and `_fill_many` do, so an on-grid prompt
filled in pairs replays too; a replay never joins a pair.

